You don't need to create a custom abstraction — **.NET 8+ ships with `System.TimeProvider`**, which is exactly this.

**In your `ReportGenerator`, inject `TimeProvider`:**

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider;

    public ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
    {
        _timeProvider = timeProvider;
    }

    public Report Generate()
    {
        var now = _timeProvider.GetUtcNow(); // instead of DateTime.UtcNow
        // ...
    }
}
```

**In production (DI registration):**

```csharp
services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

**In tests, use `FakeTimeProvider`** from `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 9, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var generator = new ReportGenerator(fake);

var report = generator.Generate();

Assert.Equal(new DateTime(2026, 1, 15, 9, 0, 0), report.GeneratedAt);

// You can also advance time:
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
```

Install the test helper package:

```
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing
```

No custom interface needed — `TimeProvider` is mockable out of the box.
